<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kapilendra Deva</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1467">1467</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kapilendra Deva was a minister and general of the last Eastern Ganga king who seized the throne of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odisha">Odisha</a> in 1434 and founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gajapati-dynasty">Suryavamsi Gajapati dynasty</a>. A devotee of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jagannath">Jagannath</a> at Puri, he fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahmani-sultanate">Bahmani Sultanate</a>, Vijayanagara and the Bengal Sultanate, and his armies extended Gajapati rule from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges</a> delta far down the eastern coast of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. His succession was disputed after his death.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kapilendra-deva.jpg" alt="Kapilendra Deva" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Prachites · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gajapati_Kapilendradeva.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Accounts of his origins differ. The Madala Panji, the chronicle of the Jagannath Temple in Puri, says he was known as Kapila Rauta, of the Surya Vamsa line, and grazed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> with a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmin</a> named Kasia, who foretold that he would be king after a cobra was seen shading his head as he rested. He later begged near the Vimala Temple at Puri and was adopted by the Eastern Ganga king Bhanudeva after a divine dream. The Raghudevapuram copper plate grant traces the family to his grandfather Kapileswara, a lieutenant in the Ganga army.</p><p>He took the throne with support inside the kingdom while Bhanu Deva IV was away on an expedition in the south, and was proclaimed with a Rājyābhiṣēka ceremony at Old Bhubaneswar, starting an era known as Kapilabda. Vassal rulers such as the Matsarvamshi of Oddadi and the Shilavamshis of Nandapur refused to accept him; he put them down by force, and the rebels were suppressed by 1440.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>His minister Gopinath Mahapatra met the threat from the Jaunpur Sultanate and drove back the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>; Gajapati forces then took lands west of the Ganga, including the fort of Gar Mandaran, and Kapilendra took the title Gaudeshwara, Lord of Gauda. In the south a first campaign of 1444 was abandoned, but in 1446 his eldest son Hamvira Deva defeated the Reddis of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajahmundry">Rajahmundry</a>, and after August 1453 his army crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a> and occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kondavidu">Kondavidu</a>. A victory over the Bahmani forces at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/devarakonda">Devarakonda</a> in 1458 made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telangana">Telangana</a> a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> state.</p><p>An invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bidar">Bidar</a> in 1461 ended in retreat when Husain Sharqi of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaunpur">Jaunpur</a> invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odisha">Odisha</a>. Hamvira Deva took the capital of Vijayanagara and made Mallikarjuna Raya pay yearly tribute, and inscriptions at Shrirangam say he advanced as far as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiruchirappalli">Tiruchirappalli</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thanjavur">Thanjavur</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arcot">Arcot</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Kapilendra called himself Routaray, the servant-king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jagannath">Jagannath</a>. He enlarged the Puri temple, built its two concentric walls, the Kurma Prachira and Meghanada Prachira, and began the Chandan Jatra festival, while also building the Kapilesvara Temple in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhubaneswar">Bhubaneswar</a>. He wrote a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> play, Parshuram Bijaya. Under him Odia was used as an administrative language and the poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarala-das">Sarala Das</a> wrote the Odia Mahabharata. He reduced taxes and warned his officials that injustice would be punished with exile.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kapilendra Deva” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Weakened by attacks from the Delhi Sultanate and the Bengal Sultanate, the last Ganga king was overthrown in 1434 by his minister <b>Kapilendra Deva</b>, who founded the Gajapati kingdom of the Suryavamshi dynasty.</blockquote><a href="/state/eastern-ganga-dynasty">Eastern Ganga dynasty</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1434 <b>Kapilendra Deva</b>, a minister and general of the last Eastern Ganga king, seized the throne at Cuttack and founded the Suryavamsi dynasty.</blockquote><a href="/state/gajapati-kingdom">Gajapati Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
